{"chunk_id": "c49b89e1c493", "text": "Takshaka plots to kill King Parikshit despite the king\u2019s precautions. Using deception, he sends snakes disguised as ascetics bearing offerings of fruit, kusa grass, and water. The king accepts the gifts and shares the fruit with his ministers. Inside one fruit hides Takshaka, appearing first as a tiny, ugly insect. Aware of the prophecy that he will die from the serpent\u2019s bite, Parikshit ironically invites the creature to become Takshaka and fulfill the curse. The insect transforms, coils around the king\u2019s neck, and fatally bites him; the palace soon burns with the serpent\u2019s poison. After the king\u2019s death, the people crown his young son Janamejaya, who later marries Vapushtama. The chapter then shifts to the ascetic Jaratkaru, who discovers his ancestors hanging over a pit, symbolizing a lineage on the verge of extinction and setting up a crucial future quest.\nTakshaka kills King Parikshit through a deceptive fruit offering; Janamejaya becomes king, and the ascetic Jaratkaru later encounters his endangered ancestors.\nThemes: fate and curse fulfillment, deception and vengeance, royal succession, ancestral duty, asceticism", "vector": [0.017440536990761757, -0.009406057186424732, -0.0022758757695555687, -0.0021632586140185595, 0.0013883850770071149, 0.014831852167844772, -0.02256377786397934, -0.022456204518675804, -0.013998148962855339, -0.012189550325274467, 0.02478250488638878, 0.002654067939147353, 0.018018750473856926, 0.01613619364798069, -0.003420537104830146, 0.0015144491335377097, 0.05577744543552399, -0.015531087294220924, 0.0306856632232666, -0.02183764986693859, 0.007543670944869518, 0.008969034999608994, 0.017319515347480774, 0.01484529860317707, -0.006094775162637234, 0.018879348412156105, -0.006252775434404612, -0.02669195458292961, 0.006128392182290554, -0.0052039227448403835, -0.010629718191921711, -0.019107943400740623, -0.012075251899659634, -0.015934491530060768, -0.039372313767671585